[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-337211"] :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y={x}^{3}{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{2x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{2}(x)=\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)={x}^{3}{e}^{5x}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{2x})^{′}-({e}^{x})^{′}({e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(2{e}^{2x})-({e}^{x})({e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{3x}-{e}^{3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}({e}^{2x})^{′}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{2}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{x}}=-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ (-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{x}){e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ -{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{e}^{5x}}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={x}^{3}{e}^{3x}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186144251 :answer-field/correct 17592186144252 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186144256 :answer-field/correct 17592186144260 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337218/field-3/1255e5fed724556b88cff85569fcce773829729bed480570e9fad97cfb82ce1c" :answer/id #uuid "73c1cbc7-3279-5ccb-ace7-12d508f30069" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337218/field-3/1f0fd8445ef9b0e2b698b7c215837b6bdd029545be90f8526e7315239c204172" :answer/id #uuid "3deb4c1e-9926-54e1-810b-efb2cc582d97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337218/field-3/34bb8a2c180982d226aa9d8874eee5012f87f5f379f2d50a25219d3590f9f781" :answer/id #uuid "debf3b8e-15da-576d-87ba-74c513409625" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337218/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "edc81094-026b-539a-9900-9417504fd7ff" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-337218/field-3/1f0fd8445ef9b0e2b698b7c215837b6bdd029545be90f8526e7315239c204172" :answer-field/choices ["q-337218/field-3/1255e5fed724556b88cff85569fcce773829729bed480570e9fad97cfb82ce1c" "q-337218/field-3/1f0fd8445ef9b0e2b698b7c215837b6bdd029545be90f8526e7315239c204172" "q-337218/field-3/34bb8a2c180982d226aa9d8874eee5012f87f5f379f2d50a25219d3590f9f781"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337218/field-4/86771dfa1d72b68103be8f82a63c2d8be53027abc2c40d3bc0e9748ba1ed8c69" :answer/id #uuid "72de5187-940c-55bf-b961-4664abdb9fee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337218/field-4/6bedfb2ec2015dfc797536bfc2c11924a22151710126a441f7e0b4b9e77e1032" :answer/id #uuid "28db3942-e17f-5a74-b90e-8afe0e90311c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337218/field-4/0eb20a896fd989ec739f26242440374e8c2769509b554769b6d1c3cbf9787c7f" :answer/id #uuid "8ec90432-0c4d-5cf7-a735-85ea8078ab4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337218/field-4/3363636aebfd7a7977c3459dd1427f95fd5c51a9f0bcb714c4c8715534e20d73" :answer/id #uuid "eda61926-7b14-595f-b959-30146c35edb0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337218/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "f0d49228-0c2d-5a0d-8181-4065b19b2556" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-337218/field-4/0eb20a896fd989ec739f26242440374e8c2769509b554769b6d1c3cbf9787c7f" :answer-field/choices ["q-337218/field-4/86771dfa1d72b68103be8f82a63c2d8be53027abc2c40d3bc0e9748ba1ed8c69" "q-337218/field-4/6bedfb2ec2015dfc797536bfc2c11924a22151710126a441f7e0b4b9e77e1032" "q-337218/field-4/0eb20a896fd989ec739f26242440374e8c2769509b554769b6d1c3cbf9787c7f" "q-337218/field-4/3363636aebfd7a7977c3459dd1427f95fd5c51a9f0bcb714c4c8715534e20d73"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-337218"] :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y={x}^{3}{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{2x}.$ \n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}={u}_{1}(x),{u}_{2}={u}_{2}(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{p}$ with respect to $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following constraint equation relating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ and ${u}_{2}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}.\n$$\n\nHere, ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for ${y}_{p},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{2x})^{′}.\n$$\n\nWe can form a constraint that relates ${u}_{1}^{′}$ and ${u}_{2}^{′}$ by differentiating ${y}_{p}$ and equating this expression to our constraint on ${y}_{p}^{′}.$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{p}$ with respect to $x$ using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={({u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{2x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({u}_{1}{e}^{x})}^{′}+{({u}_{2}{e}^{2x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}({e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we equate our two expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x} & ={u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x} & ={u}_{1}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+\\cancel{{u}_{1}{e}^{x}}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}} & =\\cancel{{u}_{1}{e}^{x}}+\\cancel{2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-337218/field-3" "q-337218/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-337332"] :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y={x}^{3}{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{2x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=-\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)={x}^{3}{e}^{5x}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{2x})^{′}-({e}^{x})^{′}({e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(2{e}^{2x})-({e}^{x})({e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{3x}-{e}^{3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{2x})}^{′}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}=-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}){e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}-2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ -{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =-{x}^{3}{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -{x}^{3}{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =-\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={x}^{3}{e}^{4x}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-337944/field-1/ae5cedf9cb811a0b92a5c60e1f208e1f479602808ee9837035b760d903d3f8bf" :answer/id #uuid "329d6a5e-7c7d-5820-a71a-bb870c55bc58" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+{u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337944/field-1/050bedcc37d4959d1ca4fca89a93be409be3842be47b764438bbcf32d55bc2ff" :answer/id #uuid "28b27848-74db-5f6f-9b3f-37f6717faf44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337944/field-1/6dc0f57b9f67f0323108d4c3984ae3d097d4fa1da3153ccc66ec6344d983e1fe" :answer/id #uuid "6eccbecd-c1b4-5f97-a790-72b0262799d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x))^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x))^{′}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337944/field-1/cbac409e13ecf446fb53efaae0f278009267dc1eb5db2c170c6b3a6c1793421e" :answer/id #uuid "771e5ec2-eaa2-5547-818e-b09edca5baf9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x))^{′}+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x))^{′}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337944/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "c19b8a73-7da6-5660-baed-0d87bca6110d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-337944/field-1/ae5cedf9cb811a0b92a5c60e1f208e1f479602808ee9837035b760d903d3f8bf" :answer-field/choices ["q-337944/field-1/ae5cedf9cb811a0b92a5c60e1f208e1f479602808ee9837035b760d903d3f8bf" "q-337944/field-1/050bedcc37d4959d1ca4fca89a93be409be3842be47b764438bbcf32d55bc2ff" "q-337944/field-1/6dc0f57b9f67f0323108d4c3984ae3d097d4fa1da3153ccc66ec6344d983e1fe" "q-337944/field-1/cbac409e13ecf446fb53efaae0f278009267dc1eb5db2c170c6b3a6c1793421e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337944/field-2/cbac409e13ecf446f